Reversing Potassium.dll: A Deep Dive into a Game Overlay Framework
Introduction
Sometimes a file lands in your lap and you know immediately that something interesting is hiding inside. Potassium.dll was one of those files. No symbols, no readable strings, a single export, and an import table so empty it looked like the author had deliberately scrubbed it clean. Spoiler: they had. What followed was a few hours of IDA Pro, some SIMD arithmetic on a notepad, and a picture that came together piece by piece — a self-contained game overlay framework with its own rendering pipeline, input binding system, scripting loader, and a surprisingly thoughtful anti-analysis layer.
This post walks through everything: the obfuscation, the string encryption, the auth and workspace initialization flow, the DirectX shader hidden in an encrypted blob, and what the whole thing tells us about how modern game overlays are built.

First Contact: The Import Table That Wasn't There
The first thing you do with any unknown DLL is look at the import table. What APIs does it call? Does it touch networking? Does it touch the filesystem? You can learn a lot in thirty seconds just from the imports.
Potassium had essentially nothing. The .idata segment was four kilobytes — a rounding error for a 3.6 MB DLL — and it contained only a single function pointer, off_180399FB0, which showed up in the DllMain dispatcher doing actual work. Everything else was absent.
That is not an accident. When you see a binary this size with no visible imports, one of a few things is happening. Either the author is using a custom loader that resolves APIs at runtime, or they are calling through a function pointer table populated at attach time, or some combination of both. In Potassium's case the answer turned out to be a pattern repeated across dozens of call sites: ((void (__fastcall *)(args))((char *)&byte_180399001 + OFFSET))(args). The base address byte_180399001 is a region inside .rdata, and each offset points to a function pointer slot populated dynamically. No symbol, no name, no import entry — just an offset into a runtime-filled table. This effectively turns IDA's cross-reference graph into a dead end at every interesting call site and makes automated analysis tools largely useless.

The XOR Key and SIMD String Encryption
No readable strings anywhere in the binary either. The .rdata section, which normally holds string literals, was full of what looked like random noise. But random noise with a pattern is not random — and once you find the decryption routine, the pattern becomes obvious.
The string initialization happens in sub_1800396F0, the largest single function in the binary at 27,551 bytes. It is called once on DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH and its job is to allocate and decrypt every string the DLL will ever use. The mechanism is consistent throughout: two 64-bit constants are loaded into XMM registers, XORed together with _mm_xor_ps, and the result written to a freshly heap-allocated buffer via a custom allocator (sub_180385FC0). The resulting pointer is stored in a global variable for later use.
The key is 0x8C34440187AEB275 in the low 64 bits and 0x97938BF4146AD3EA in the high 64 bits, making it a 128-bit value applied across 16 bytes at a time. Using SSE instructions for this is a deliberate choice — it looks like floating-point math to a naive disassembler, the operations are fast, and it breaks simple string-search-based analysis. You cannot grep for the strings, you cannot use a tool like strings on the binary, and you cannot easily pattern-match the constants without first understanding what they are a key to.
Decrypting the table manually is straightforward once you have the key. You take each encrypted 64-bit pair, XOR both halves against the respective key half, interpret the result as a null-terminated string up to the declared length, and you get the plaintext. Running all of them through gives you the complete picture of what Potassium actually knows about:
The full input name table: Tab, LeftArrow, RightArrow, UpArrow, DownArrow, PageDown, Home, End, Insert, Delete, Backspace, Space, Enter, every modifier key (LeftCtrl through RightSuper), every bracket and punctuation key, CapsLock, ScrollLock, NumLock, Pause, all nine Keypad variants, KeypadDecimal through KeypadEqual, MouseLeft, MouseRight, ModShift, ModSuper, AppBack, AppForward, GamepadStart, GamepadBack, and a sequence of Gamepad function button names.
This is not a random collection of strings. This is a complete, systematically organized input binding table — the kind you would build if you were implementing a scripting API where users bind actions to key names by string. It matches almost exactly the key name table used in ImGui's input system.
A second key (0xC94C7C78B801448F | 0x923223F9F796295C) is used in a separate context for filesystem path strings, decrypting to: "Potassium", "LOCALAPPDATA", "workspace", and "autoexec".

The Integrity Canary
Before any initialization happens, DllEntryPoint makes a call to sub_180386B00. This is a self-integrity check. It verifies a magic constant (0x2B992DDFA232) stored in qword_18047FCC0, then derives a new value using a combination of the stack address of a local variable, an XOR with a value from RDTSC-adjacent calls through the dynamic dispatch table, and some bitwise gymnastics. The result is stored back alongside its bitwise complement in qword_18047FD00.
The pattern — storing both a value and ~value — is a classic tamper detection technique. At any point the DLL can check whether the two globals are still bitwise complements of each other. If they are not, something has written into that memory region unexpectedly. If a debugger has patched a byte, if a memory scanner has stomped on the region, if a hook has redirected the function that sets the value — the check will fail. The value 0x2B992DDFA233 is explicitly produced and then rejected: the code adds one to the expected magic and then checks whether the derived value equals the original, storing the incremented version if it does to ensure the comparison is always slightly off. It is subtle but deliberate.

The Auth and Workspace Flow
The real attach handler is sub_1801BA670. When fdwReason == DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH, this function runs the full initialization sequence. Walking through it:
It saves the hInstDLL handle passed to DllEntryPoint into a global. It stores a second parameter — presumably a configuration structure — into qword_1804A8D48. It calls sub_1801BA360, which sets up additional internal state.
Then it starts building filesystem paths. It decrypts "LOCALAPPDATA" and resolves the environment variable, giving it the user's local application data directory. It appends the decrypted string "Potassium" to get the application data folder, then appends "workspace" to get the working directory. It decrypts "autoexec" and constructs the full path to the autoexec file inside that workspace directory.
The function then calls into what appears to be a file-loading routine, reads the autoexec content, evaluates it, and stores the result in two global std::wstring objects (xmmword_180499C20 and xmmword_180499C40). There is explicit reference counting and string capacity management around these globals — the code checks whether the existing buffer capacity is sufficient before reusing it, and calls into the allocator when it needs to grow.
After loading the autoexec, there is a conditional branch checking the result. If loading fails in a specific way, the function still returns 1 (success to the system) but skips the subsequent initialization steps. If it succeeds, it triggers what appears to be a rendering context setup and schedules further work.
The overall pattern is a plugin system. The DLL installs itself, locates a user-controlled script file at a known path, executes it, and uses the result to configure behavior. The specific path %LOCALAPPDATA%\Potassium\workspace\autoexec is effectively the user-facing API.

The Hidden DirectX Shader
The second largest function, sub_180040290 at 6,720 bytes, does something that initially looks bizarre: it spends hundreds of instructions loading XMM registers with large constants, XORing pairs together, and writing results into a 735-byte heap allocation. This is the same XOR encryption pattern as the string table, but operating on a much larger blob with a wider variety of keys.
Decrypting it sequentially — each 128-bit block XORed against a corresponding 128-bit key from the second half of the function's stack frame — produces readable text starting from the very first block:
cbuffer vertexBuffer : register(b0)
{
    float4x4 ProjectionMatrix;
};
struct VS_INPUT
{
    float2 pos : POSITION;
    float4 col : COLOR0;
    float2 uv : TEXCOORD0;
};
That is a DirectX 11 HLSL vertex shader. The cbuffer with a projection matrix, the VS_INPUT structure with position, color, and UV coordinates — this is the standard vertex layout for a 2D overlay renderer. If you have ever looked at Dear ImGui's DirectX 11 example backend, this vertex shader is nearly character-for-character identical to the one ImGui ships.
Potassium includes its own GPU rendering pipeline, embedded in encrypted form, decoded at runtime, and compiled through the DirectX shader compiler API (presumably called through the dynamic dispatch table, which is why there is no D3DCompile import visible). This means it renders directly to the screen surface without relying on any external rendering library.

Putting It All Together
Potassium.dll is a complete game overlay framework. It loads from a predictable path in the user's profile, executes a user-controlled autoexec script, and renders to screen using its own DirectX shader pipeline. The input binding table covers every key and mouse button a user might want to bind a script action to. The obfuscation — XOR string encryption, SIMD decryption, dynamic API dispatch through an offset table, and an integrity canary — is not the work of someone who stumbled into it; it is deliberate, layered, and consistent.
From a reverse engineering standpoint the anti-analysis layer is more interesting than complex. None of the individual techniques are novel, but the combination is effective against automated tooling and makes manual analysis considerably slower. The SIMD XOR in particular is a nice touch — it looks like vectorized math, it runs in a fraction of the time a loop would, and it completely defeats string extraction at the binary level.
The workspace/autoexec model is clean from a user-experience perspective. Users drop scripts into a known directory, the DLL finds and runs them on load, and the input system gives those scripts a vocabulary of named keys to bind to. It is the same mental model as classic Lua loaders in game mods, just packaged as a DLL with its own rendering layer on top.
Nothing in the binary suggests network communication, license checking, or telemetry — at least not in the code paths analyzed here. Whether those exist elsewhere in the 500-function codebase is an open question.
